From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: a STA-MLD on a backhaul BSS is a backhaul
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

sta_mld.is_bsta was hardcoded to 0 on both the insert and the update branch,
so no STA-MLD was ever recorded as a backhaul. Every backhaul link therefore
appeared in the client tables as an ordinary station that never leaves.

In a chain they are spread over the nodes that host them; in a star they
would all pile onto the controller. Either way it is one bogus client per
hop, and it grows with the mesh.

Nothing new has to be plumbed in: the AP-MLD the station is associated to is
already bound, and the database already records which BSSes are backhaul.

It has to be looked up through affiliated_ap rather than straight in bss:
ap_mld_macaddr is an MLD address while bss holds per-LINK BSSIDs, and the two
coincide only where link 0 happens to carry the MLD address. Measured against
the live database on 2026-08-04, the direct lookup resolved
86:f7:56:41:01:d4 and silently returned 0 for ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:af - it would
have fixed one backhaul and left the other showing as a client. Through
affiliated_ap both resolve to 1 and the fronthaul client stays 0. The direct
lookup is kept as a fallback for a plain, non-MLD AP.

Numbered parameters keep the existing bind indices untouched. If the rows
have not been seen yet the expression yields 0, which is exactly the current
behaviour, and the next sync corrects it.

--- a/src/db/mapc_sync.c	2026-08-04 18:32:45.963574075 +0200
+++ b/src/db/mapc_sync.c	2026-08-04 18:32:45.963618833 +0200
@@ -759,12 +759,43 @@
 	 * columns do not arbitrate on their own; they record who claimed what
 	 * and when, so the caller can tell a fresh claim from a fading one
 	 * (and so the DB can finally answer "where is this client"). */
+	/* is_bsta was hardcoded to 0 on both branches, so no STA-MLD was ever
+	 * recorded as a backhaul and every backhaul link showed up in the
+	 * client tables as an ordinary station that never leaves. In a chain
+	 * they are spread over the nodes that host them; in a star they would
+	 * all pile onto the controller. Either way it is one bogus client per
+	 * hop, and it grows with the mesh.
+	 *
+	 * Nothing new has to be plumbed in to know better: the AP-MLD the
+	 * station is associated to is already bound (parameter 7), and the
+	 * database already knows which BSSes are backhaul.
+	 *
+	 * It has to be looked up through affiliated_ap, not straight in bss:
+	 * ap_mld_macaddr is an MLD address while bss holds per-LINK BSSIDs, and
+	 * the two coincide only on the nodes where link 0 happens to carry the
+	 * MLD address. Measured 2026-08-04: 86:f7:56:41:01:d4 was in bss and
+	 * resolved, ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:af was not and silently returned 0 - one
+	 * backhaul fixed, the other still shown as a client. The direct lookup
+	 * stays as a fallback for a plain, non-MLD AP.
+	 *
+	 * Numbered parameters keep the existing bind indices untouched. If the
+	 * rows have not been seen yet this yields 0, exactly today's behaviour,
+	 * and the next sync corrects it. */
 	static const char *ins_stamld =
 		"INSERT INTO sta_mld(mld_macaddr, is_bsta, emlmr_enabled, "
 		"emlsr_enabled, str_enabled, nstr_enabled, num_sta, "
 		"ap_mld_macaddr, last_seen) "
-		"VALUES(?,0,?,?,?,?,?,?,?) "
-		"ON CONFLICT(mld_macaddr) DO UPDATE SET is_bsta=0, "
+		"VALUES(?1,"
+		"COALESCE((SELECT MAX(b.is_bbss) FROM affiliated_ap a "
+		"JOIN bss b ON b.bssid=a.bssid WHERE a.mld_macaddr=?7),"
+		"(SELECT MAX(is_bbss) FROM bss WHERE bssid=?7),0),"
+		"?2,?3,?4,?5,?6,?7,?8) "
+		"ON CONFLICT(mld_macaddr) DO UPDATE SET "
+		"is_bsta=COALESCE((SELECT MAX(b.is_bbss) FROM affiliated_ap a "
+		"JOIN bss b ON b.bssid=a.bssid "
+		"WHERE a.mld_macaddr=excluded.ap_mld_macaddr),"
+		"(SELECT MAX(is_bbss) FROM bss "
+		"WHERE bssid=excluded.ap_mld_macaddr),0), "
 		"emlmr_enabled=excluded.emlmr_enabled, "
 		"emlsr_enabled=excluded.emlsr_enabled, "
 		"str_enabled=excluded.str_enabled, "
